// @run
// @log s
// @log 4
// The MODULE-MODE cell of the exported-generic-at-two-types question, and it answers the
// same as the single-file `functions/exported-generic-two-types-export-order.vl`: the ABI
// entry is the FIRST instantiation's shape, chosen by source order of the call sites.
//
// Here the string call comes first, so the re-exported `id` publishes
// `((ref 0)) -> ((ref 0))` (measured on the export section). Swapping the two `print`s
// publishes `(i32) -> i32` instead — the merge puts both call sites in one module, so
// module mode adds no new answer, which is the point of pinning it.
//
// The export is truthful, not wrong: host-calling it at the published type returns the
// identity, and the other instantiation is a separate private function no importer can
// name. What an importer CANNOT do is reach the string instantiation and the i32 one
// through one exported name — a wasm export names exactly one functype. That narrowing is
// the real, permanent consequence of exporting a generic, and it is why the fix for a
// program that needs both is concrete wrappers, not a change here.
import { id } from "./lib"

export { id } from "./lib"

print(id("s"))
print(id(4))
